# Key trust: what the padlock on a direct message is entitled to claim, and the ceremony that
# lets it claim more.
#
# Three things to watch, in the order they appear:
#
#   1. The transcript. Foxtrot Mobile's key is unverified, so its direct message carries the
#      padlock it always did. Then the same bubble with the key proven - the mark becomes a
#      shield, which is a shape rather than a hue, because at this size two tints of one glyph
#      are not a difference a thumb-held screen can carry. Nothing about the older message is
#      re-drawn: it was always encrypted to that key, and what moved is whether anybody has
#      established whose it is.
#   2. Bravo Creek's detail, with its key proven and the radio's own database having lost it.
#      The Identity card ends with Key - the fingerprint, and under it the word for what that
#      fingerprint is worth, in the one ink on this screen that is a judgement rather than a
#      reading. The two rows that change it sit last under Actions, after everything that acts
#      on the node's traffic: "Put back on radio" appears only for a node the radio has
#      forgotten, which is that row's whole job.
#   3. The sheet, at each of its stages. Every one of them keeps a paragraph, and that is not
#      decoration: what makes this ceremony proof of anything is that the number and the code
#      travel by voice, and the paragraph is the only thing on the panel that says so.

scene demo
scale 4
delay 200
clock 2025-06-14 14:05

# ---- the padlock, before and after ----------------------------------------------------------

tab messages
key down 3
key a
hold 1600

verified FXTR
hold 2000

key b
hold 500

# ---- the rows behind the mark ---------------------------------------------------------------

verified BRVO
offradio BRVO
tab nodes
# The Nodes list has three rows before its first node - the filter row, the sort row and
# the Map (MESH_UI_NODES_LEAD_ROWS) - so a node's row is its index in the demo roster plus
# three. Bravo Creek is seeds[2] and row 5.
key down 5
key a
hold 1200

# Down through the action rows to the two that are about the key. They are last because one of
# them opens a ceremony involving two people and a telephone, and that is not something to land
# on by overshooting a cursor.
key down 10
hold 1800

# And into Identity, where the fingerprint and the word for it sit at the end of the card.
key down 5
hold 2000

key b
hold 500

# ---- the ceremony ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Asked, and the two radios are working it out. "Later" gets out of the way without ending
# anything; the sheet comes back when the radio next asks for something.
verify waiting BRVO
hold 2200

# The end that did *not* start it is shown four digits to read out loud. The digits are the
# headline, drawn at the panel's largest, because they are the whole of what this screen is for.
verify show BRVO
hold 2400

# The end that did start it types what it heard. A keyboard rather than a sheet - it is the one
# stage that collects something - and the same prompt shape the BLE pairing PIN uses, for the
# same reason: the radio asked, in the middle of whatever the user was doing. Four digits and no
# more; the counter says so and the fifth keypress does nothing.
verify enter BRVO
hold 1600
key right 3
key a
key right
key a
key right
key a
key right
key a
# A fifth keypress on a field the radio only ever generates four digits for: the counter stays
# at 4/4 and nothing is appended, because a number refused where it is typed beats one the radio
# will not match.
key right
key a
hold 1800

# Both ends now show the same short code. Both answers are answers here, and the fill says which
# one A would press.
verify compare BRVO
hold 2400
key right
hold 1800

# And on the palette with one accent and no held-back version of it, where the two answers
# cannot be told apart by hue - which is why the fill moves instead of being shared.
theme contrast
hold 2000
theme light
hold 2000
